    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.git\n\n### Description\n\nSometimes I just want to write a short message, I will use `Git commit -m \"some messages\"`. But mini.git cannot run that, and throw error `(mini.git) pathspec did not match any files known to git`\n\n### Neovim version\n\nNVIM v0.11.0-dev-1210+g3597633075\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim -c \"lua require('mini.git').setup()\"`\r\n2. Go to a Git repository that has been modified\r\n3. Run `:Git commit -m \"my messages\"`\r\n4. It will throw an error\r\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nI want it to behave like vim-fugitive, it just run the command and returns the same output as I run `git commit -m \"my messages\"` in terminal\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n`(mini.git) pathspec 'messages\"' did not match any files known to git`",
